---
// Terms of service.
//
// SCOPE IS THE WHOLE DESIGN OF THIS PAGE. It governs use of the WEBSITE and the
// things the website does — reading it, asking the assistant, booking a call,
// submitting an application, taking the published resources. It deliberately
// does NOT set the commercial terms of the cohort or of a consulting
// engagement. Those are agreed in writing at the time, and this page says so
// and stops.
//
// WHY THAT LINE IS WHERE IT IS, AND DO NOT MOVE IT WITHOUT ASKING SUNIL.
// On 2026-08-28 he decided not to publish withdrawal or refund terms for the
// first cohort. Payment timing is public (`cohort.paymentDue` in facts.ts);
// what happens if someone pulls out is not, and that silence is deliberate
// rather than an oversight. A terms page is the obvious place for that silence
// to get filled in by habit, because every template has a refunds clause. This
// one does not. The single sentence below saying those terms arrive with a seat
// offer was written to answer "where do I find this" without publishing the
// policy or inviting the question.
//
// Facts restated here come from src/data/facts.ts and must not drift from it:
// applying costs nothing and commits you to nothing; the assessment fee is
// credited toward the first month of a retainer begun within 60 days; the
// published resources need no account, licence or email address.
import PolicyLayout from '../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../data/site';
import { canonical } from '../data/facts';

export const prerender = false;

const title = 'Terms of service — The Living Craft';
const description =
  'The terms for using this website, its assistant, its booking and its published resources. The commercial terms of a programme or an engagement are agreed separately, in writing.';

const UPDATED = '14 September 2026';
---

<PolicyLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical('/terms')} label="Terms">
  <section class="policy-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow"><span class="num">02</span> Terms</span>
      <h1>Terms for using this site</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        These cover the website and what it does: reading it, asking the assistant, booking a call,
        applying for a seat, and taking the material published here. They do not set the price or
        the conditions of any programme or engagement. Those are agreed with you directly, in
        writing.
      </p>
      <p class="stamp">Last updated {UPDATED}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="policy">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="summary">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Use the site sensibly and it is yours to use, free, with no account.</li>
          <li>The assistant can be wrong. Nothing it says is advice, and nothing it says is an offer.</li>
          <li>Applying costs nothing and commits you to nothing. A seat exists only once I offer one and you accept it.</li>
          <li>Booking a call is a diary entry, not a contract. Either of us can move or cancel it.</li>
          <li>The published resources are free to use and to pass on, including at work.</li>
          <li>What a programme or an engagement costs, and the terms attached to it, come to you in writing. They are not on this page.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>Who you are dealing with</h2>
      <p>
        This site is run by Sunil Mathew, operating as The Living Craft, from Bangalore, India.
        There is no company behind it and no team. Write to
        <a class="ftext-link" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and I answer it
        myself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Using this site means you accept what is on this page. If you do not, the remedy is simple
        and costs you nothing: stop using it.
      </p>

      <h2>What these terms do not cover</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The commercial terms of the cohort and of consulting work are not set here.</strong>
        They are agreed with you directly, at the point there is something to agree. For the cohort
        that means the offer you receive if you are given a seat. For consulting it means a written
        scope and engagement agreement signed by both of us.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where those documents and this page ever disagree about a programme or an engagement, those
        documents govern. This page governs the website.
      </p>

      <h2>Using the site</h2>
      <p>
        You may read everything here, quote it with attribution, and share the links. You do not
        need an account, and there is nothing to sign up for.
      </p>
      <p>Please do not:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>submit somebody else's details, or book a call in somebody else's name</li>
        <li>attempt to reach the sign-in areas, the operator console, or any data that is not yours</li>
        <li>scrape the site at a rate that costs me money, or run automated load against the assistant</li>
        <li>use the assistant to generate content unrelated to this practice, which is what the rate limits are for</li>
        <li>republish the teaching material as your own, or resell it</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I may block access that does any of these. There is no appeals process, because there is one
        of me — but if you think a block is wrong, write and I will look at it.
      </p>

      <h2>The assistant</h2>
      <p>
        The question-and-answer assistant is built on a large language model. It is given a fixed
        set of facts about this practice and instructed to say it does not know rather than guess.
        That instruction usually holds. It is not a guarantee.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Nothing the assistant says is professional advice, and nothing it says is a binding
        offer.</strong> If it states a price, a date or a seat count that turns out to differ from
        what is on the relevant page, the page is correct and the assistant is wrong. Please tell me
        when that happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not type anything confidential into it. Your question is sent to a model provider in
        order to answer it, and it is stored here so I can see what my pages failed to explain. The
        <a class="ftext-link" href="/privacy">privacy page</a> sets out exactly what that means.
      </p>

      <h2>Booking a call</h2>
      <p>
        A booked call is a diary entry. It is not a contract, it costs nothing, and it obliges
        neither of us to go further.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your invitation carries a link that moves or cancels the call. Please use it rather than
        letting a slot go empty, because the slots are finite and someone else could have taken it.
        I may also move or cancel a call, in which case you are told through the same calendar
        invitation. If I cancel and you still want the conversation, write to me and we will find
        another time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The link in that invitation is personal to your booking and lets whoever holds it change the
        call. Do not forward it.
      </p>

      <h2>Applying for a seat</h2>
      <p>
        Admission to the cohort is by application, and I read every one myself.
        <strong>Applying costs nothing and commits you to nothing.</strong> Submitting an
        application is not a purchase, and it does not reserve a seat.
      </p>
      <p>
        A seat exists only when I offer you one and you accept it. The fee, when it is payable, and
        the terms that go with it are set out in that offer. Admission is at my discretion, and most
        applications get a no.
      </p>
      <p>
        No card, bank or payment details are collected anywhere on this site. There is nothing on
        it that takes money. What each form asks for is listed in full on the
        <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are told on screen whether your application was saved, and shown a reference. That
        message is the confirmation. No automated email is sent, so do not wait for one.
      </p>
      <p>
        No response time is published. I have not agreed one, and publishing a target nobody has
        committed to would be a promise rather than a term.
      </p>

      <h2>Consulting work</h2>
      <p>
        The pages describing the Fractional Chief AI Officer retainer and the AI Readiness
        Assessment are descriptions of what I do, not offers capable of acceptance. Any engagement
        starts with a written scope agreed by both of us, which is where the fee, the duration, the
        deliverables and everything else are fixed.
      </p>
      <p>
        One published commitment does carry over, because it is stated on both pages: if you
        commission an AI Readiness Assessment and begin a fractional CAIO engagement within 60 days
        of it, the full assessment fee is credited toward your first month.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prices shown on this site are indicative and are confirmed on a scope call before anything
        is agreed.
      </p>

      <h2>The material published here</h2>
      <p>
        The writing, the teaching material and the site itself are mine, and copyright stays with
        me. The published resources are a deliberate exception, and the permission is broad: they
        are free to use and free to pass on, including inside your own organisation and in your own
        work. Nothing there needs an account, a licence or an email address.
      </p>
      <p>
        What that permission does not include is republishing the material as your own, or selling
        it. Attribution is appreciated and not demanded.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are a cohort participant, what you write and build during the programme is yours. I
        do not claim ownership of your work, and I do not publish it without asking you first.
      </p>

      <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
      <p>
        Some pages cite or link to other people's work. Those sites are not mine, I do not control
        what they publish, and a link is not an endorsement of everything on the other end of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        This is a small site run by one person. It may be unavailable for maintenance, because a
        service it depends on is down, or because I broke something. <strong>There is no uptime
        commitment</strong>, and features may change or be removed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a part of the site fails, the rest is designed to keep working — a booking still saves
        when the calendar service is unreachable, and the assistant points you at a form when it
        cannot answer. That is the intent rather than a promise.
      </p>

      <h2>Liability</h2>
      <p>
        The site and its material are provided as they are. I have taken care to make them accurate
        and useful, and I do not warrant that they are free of errors or suitable for any particular
        purpose of yours.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here is professional, legal, financial or regulatory advice. The material discusses
        regulated-industry obligations because that is the subject matter, and reading it is not a
        substitute for advice about your own situation.
      </p>
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows, I am not liable for indirect or consequential loss arising
        from your use of this site, including lost profit, lost data or business interruption.
        Nothing in these terms limits liability that cannot lawfully be limited, including for
        fraud, or for death or personal injury caused by negligence.
      </p>

      <h2>Your personal data</h2>
      <p>
        Set out in full on the <a class="ftext-link" href="/privacy">privacy page</a>, which names
        every field collected, every company that processes it, how long it is kept, and how to have
        it deleted.
      </p>

      <h2>Governing law</h2>
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the laws of India, and the courts at Bengaluru have exclusive
        jurisdiction over any dispute arising from them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before that becomes relevant, write to me. Most things are a misunderstanding and are
        cheaper to fix in an email.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this page</h2>
      <p>
        These terms change as the site does, and the date at the top is the date they last changed.
        Continuing to use the site after a change means the current version applies. There is no
        archive of earlier versions and no mailing list to announce changes on.
      </p>

      <div class="closing">
        <p>
          <strong>Last updated {UPDATED}.</strong> Questions about any of this go to
          <a class="ftext-link" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, and I answer
          them myself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PolicyLayout>

<style>
  /* Same prose system as the privacy page. Duplicated rather than shared
     because two policy pages do not justify a stylesheet, and the day one of
     them needs to look different this is where that happens. */
  .policy-head { padding: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-6); }
  .policy-head h1 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-4); max-width: 18ch; }
  .policy-head .lead { max-width: var(--measure-prose); }

  .stamp {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--size-1);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    color: var(--text-quiet);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .policy { padding-bottom: var(--space-8); }
  .policy .wrap > * { max-width: var(--measure-prose); }

  .policy h2 {
    font-size: var(--size-6);
    font-weight: var(--weight-heavy);
    color: var(--text-loud);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-4);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
  }

  .policy p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); line-height: var(--lh-normal); }

  .policy ul { margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-5); }
  .policy li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); line-height: var(--lh-snug); }

  /* Panel law: a shadow or a border, never both. */
  .summary {
    background: var(--surface-panel);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-raise);
    padding: var(--space-6);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .summary h2 { margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); font-size: var(--size-5); }
  .summary ul { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .closing {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-top: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--border-w-hair) solid var(--border-hair);
  }

  .closing p { margin: 0; color: var(--text-quiet); }
</style>
